<header><p class="st-kicker">imperial city · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Free Imperial City of Kaysersberg</h1><p class="st-meta">1293 CE – 1679 CE (approx.) · capital Kaysersberg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Alsatian wine town beneath a Hohenstaufen castle that was a free imperial city and Decapolis member until French annexation.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The castle of Kaysersberg was built in the 1220s for Frederick II to guard the route from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a> into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-lorraine">Lorraine</a> over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vosges">Vosges</a>, and a town grew at its foot. It received imperial-city privileges in 1293 and in 1354 joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/decapolis">Decapolis</a>, the defensive league of ten Alsatian imperial cities under the emperor's bailiff at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-haguenau">Haguenau</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Ruled by a council and mayor, Kaysersberg was a modest community of vintners, craftsmen and traders whose wealth came from the wine of the surrounding slopes. The preacher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-geiler-von-kaysersberg">Johann Geiler of Kaysersberg</a>, the most famous pulpit voice of late medieval <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-strasbourg">Strasbourg</a>, grew up there and took his name from the town, which remained Catholic.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Like the other <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/decapolis">Decapolis</a> cities it came under French protection after 1648, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaties-of-nijmegen">Treaty of Nijmegen</a> in 1679 completed its incorporation into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-schweitzer">Albert Schweitzer</a> was born there in 1875. It is now a small town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haut-rhin">Haut-Rhin</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="imperial city, 1293 CE – 1679 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox